  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1034 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nA 596 dam ridge remains near the ArkLaTex as of Friday evening.\nThis ridge will continue to slowly sink southwest as troughing\ndigs over the Pacific Northwest. Due to the intensity of the\nridge, the area wide Heat Advisory remains in effect through 8 PM\ntomorrow. The trough will gradually impinge on the ridge\nprojecting heights to fall to around 590 dam by Sunday morning.\nSunday, heights are projected to fall below 590 dam resulting in\nlow confidence in needing a Heat Advisory for Sunday. Dewpoints\nare trending towards the low to mid 60s, but air temperatures\nwill continue to be in the upper 90s to lower 100s which brings\nRH values near critical thresholds (near 20%). It does appear\nwinds will remain light enough to alleviate wildfire danger, but\nwith ongoing drought, there is a marginal concern for any small\ngrass fires.\n\nA subtle synoptic change is favored for early next week as the\naforementioned trough targets a weak cold front at the Mid-South\non Monday. This will bring isolated to scattered PoPs into the\nforecast during the afternoon and evening, but appreciable\nrainfall is not anticipated with this activity. Probabilities of\nreceiving 0.25\" of rainfall are less than 20%. Subtle ridging\nwill build in quickly behind the boundary, but so far guidance\ndoes not depict an anomalous ridge as we are currently\nexperiencing.\n\nWednesday could be the first hint at 'relief', though still 8-10 \ndegrees above normal for the end of September. NBM percentiles \nhave a large spread, 9-12 degree differences between the 25th and \n75th percentile; so most of the guidance is favoring a slight \ncooldown. This seems to be due to troughing being projected set up\nacross the eastern CONUS. If the current forecast pans out, we \nwill be 10-15 degrees cooler during the day than what we have been\nexperiencing the entire month of September.\n\nDNM",
